  "query_preview": "..Today...Southwesterly winds will begin to increase \nlater this morning ahead of an approaching cold front. For most of\nthe area, winds are expected to be stronger than we saw yesterday,\nand as such a Wind Advisory is in effect for much of northern \nArizona today. Gusts upwards of 40-50 mph are expected across the \nhigher terrain today, with some locally higher gusts possible just\ndownwind of terrain features. Winds will likely peak later this \nafternoon into the early evening, as the upper-level jet max moves\noverhead. Winds may remain elevated past sunset set as a result, \nas model soundings do not show the boundary layer decoupling right\naway.\n\nWith the strong winds and dry conditions, widespread critical fire \nweather conditions are also expected across far northern and eastern \nArizona. A Red Flag Warning is in effect through this evening for \nthese locations. Elsewhere, while the expected winds and fuels met \ncriteria, a subtle uptick in moisture south and west of the Mogollon \nRim looks to keep RH values above the 15% threshold this afternoon. \nThus, while some localized areas of critical fire weather conditions \nwill remain possible in these areas, the threat does not appear \nwidespread enough to support headlines.\n\nTonight and Monday...As the aforementioned front moves through the \nregion later tonight, a brief period of showers looks to develop \nfrom west to east, primarily across far northern Arizona. Given that \nsurface moisture will remain fairly minimal, much of the activity is \nexpected to be either virga or light in nature. The best shot at \nmeasurable precip looks to mainly be across the Kaibab Plateau, an \npotentially Black Mesa and the Chuska Mountains as well. 00Z HREF \nguidance appears to be a bit bullish with precipitation chances, \nbrining them as far south of as the Mogollon Rim. However, model \nsoundings don't really support this outside of a subtle increase in \nlow-level moisture early Monday morning. Nevertheless, PoP chances \nwere maintained in the Flagstaff area for the potential for a couple \nisolated showers. Colder air aloft does move in with the front, so a \nfew snow flakes may mix in at times above 7000-7500 feet. However \nany potential for measurable snow looks to remain above 7500-8000 \nfeet and primarily near the Kaibab Plateau.\n\nTuesday through Saturday...Zonal flow sets in behind the low earlier \nin the week. Warming temperatures and dry conditions look to ensue \nas a result, with a return to near-normal highs by the middle to end \nof next week. Weak troughing will keep winds fairly light each day, \nwith mainly only the typical afternoon breezes expected.",
